AB  - Current large language models only capture surface patterns in code repair, ignoring the root causes of defects and global security constraints. This often results in patches passing tests but containing semantic errors or introducing new vulnerabilities. This paper proposes a causal-aware semantic safety repair model. Taking source code as input, it first constructs a program causal graph of explicitly encoded data and control dependencies. A graph attention network is then used to perform causal attribution along dependency edges, extracting the minimum necessary intervention node chain that triggers the defect. The repair intention of this chain is injected as a conditional signal into the latent space of the decoder of a pre-trained code-based large language model. A causal calibration layer maximizes the mutual information between generated lexical units and intervention nodes step by step in the decoding process to suppress patches that deviate from the root cause. Simultaneously, a Siamese program dependency graph encoder maps the code before and after repair to a semantic dependency graph summary and calculates the semantic offset. When the offset exceeds a safety threshold, the backpropagation gradient is fed into the decoder to trigger a backtracking and regeneration, forming a closed loop of “generation—verification—constraint correction”. Experimental results on the Defects4J benchmark show that the correctness of the patch function reaches 83.6 %, the security vulnerability introduction rate is reduced to 3.9 %, and the semantic consistency deviation between the patch and the original code is controlled within 0.14. This model is generated under constraints from both causal root cause and semantic integrity levels, achieving automated patching that is both functionally correct and securely reliable.
