AB  - Existing advances of agentic AI techniques largely depend on public benchmarks, while little is known about the process of evaluating them in realistic environments. We report our experience of applying LLM-based agents to automatically patch CI test failures in SAP HANA, a large C++ database system with resource-intensive CI pipelines. Instead of fault localization or patch generation techniques themselves, we focus on practical problems we encountered. First, curating a ground-truth dataset from real CI failure history can be extremely difficult. Abandoned fixes, rebased diffs, and mislabeled failures all get in the way of obtaining clean data. Second, providing useful context to LLM agents is challenging, especially when the tests are Python/SQL scripts that interact with the C++ engine only through a query interface. Third, reliable evaluation of generated patches is expensive and time-consuming, since test flakiness can produce both false positives and false negatives. We hope our experience report can help others working in CI-level agentic repair technique in similar environments.
