AB  - The practical deployment of Automated Program Repair (APR) is impeded by the difficulty of accurately assessing patch correctness. Existing techniques struggle with two critical challenges: distinguishing latent runtime semantic inconsistencies to filter out plausible but non-generalizable overfitting patches, and preventing the erroneous rejection of syntactically diverse correct repairs that deviate from standard patterns. To address these issues, we propose FeaR-CoT, a framework that deeply fuses fine-grained dynamic execution traces with static features via a serialized language representation. By incorporating a retrieval-based Chain-of-Thought (CoT) strategy, our approach empowers Large Language Models (LLMs) to perceive subtle behavioral anomalies and infer semantic equivalence across structural variations. Experimental results on widely used overfitting-patch dataset show that FeaR-CoT improves accuracy by 4% and F1-score by 1.2% over the state-of-the-art (SOTA) method. Compared with dynamic approaches constrained by test generation quality and traditional static techniques, FeaR-CoT achieves consistently high precision while delivering an exceptionally high recall of 0.989.
