AB  - The article explores transformer-based natural language processing to further the development of automated bug report classification in software repositories, overcoming the shortcomings of previous studies which mostly utilized a combination of a static text classification model or task-specific pre-trained transformers. Although previous research concentrated on coarse-grained categorization, vulnerability detection, deep learning project bug identification, or code repair, the presented work presents a dual-phase framework in which both contextual and adaptive and temporal aspects of bug management are combined. It presents a transformer-based model, which involves inputs of multi-source such as bug titles, description, reproduction-steps, and metadata that are integrated together by an adaptive fusion of attention mechanism. The research shows that this richer representation is more effective in increasing classification accuracy than traditional transformer fine-tuning methods by retrieving linguistic and structural hints in reports. It is based on this that will elaborate on the way automated classification systems can be developed over time by learning repository-aware. The analysis of temporal drift is used to track changes in the distribution of bugs, whereas the developer-in-the-loop feedback ensures never-ending improvement. Self-corrective attention mechanism is also used to dynamically re-weight contextual features by prediction confidence and historic classification patterns. According to the research, this changing framework does not only increase the long-term resilience and flexibility but also builds a scalable paradigm of intelligent and context-sensitive bug triaging in contemporary software engineering ecosystems.
ER  - 


