AB  - Multi-agent large language model (LLM) systems are increasingly used for software engineering tasks, yet the role of communication architecture in these systems remains poorly understood. Most existing approaches assume message passing, where agents exchange sequential natural-language summaries, without examining the information loss this assumption may introduce. This paper makes a methodological and diagnostic contribution to that question. We present SE-Blackboard, a modular framework that places message passing, a shared-state blackboard, and a hybrid design within an otherwise identical multi-agent pipeline, and we introduce the Information Fidelity Score (IFS), a stage-level metric that measures how well key technical entities from the original issue survive successive agent handoffs. On 50 SWE-bench Lite issues, the blackboard configuration improves Coder-stage IFS by 62% over message passing (0.586 vs. 0.362) and correct file targeting by 27.5 percentage points (82.6% vs. 55.1%). Once the correct file is identified, however, all configurations converge to a ~22% conditional resolve rate, revealing a downstream bottleneck in LLM diff generation. Building on these decompositions, we present an information-flow bottleneck model that predicts how upstream gains attenuate at the patch-generation stage; the model accounts for the modest end-to-end resolve-rate improvement observed (16% vs. 12%). These findings identify communication architecture as a measurable, separable design dimension and supply tools—IFS and the bottleneck model—for diagnosing where future improvements in multi-agent SE pipelines should be targeted.
ER  - 


