AB  - Learning Management Systems (LMS) are the backbone of modern digital education but are unfortunately extremely susceptible to both security attacks (e.g. SQL injection, XSS) and non-security bugs (e.g. inefficient queries, maintainability bugs). Existing static analysis tools, machine learning (ML), deep learning (DL) and general-purpose Large Language Models (LLM) are found to be less effective in LMS environments because of domain mismatch and lack of contextual awareness. This paper presents a Domain-Specific Large Language Model (DS-LLM) system for LMS software intended by fine-tuning transformer-based models using LMS specific codebases, vulnerability datasets and remediation examples. The framework combines the supervised vulnerability classification, an unsupervised anomaly detection and reinforcement learning-based patch generation that is augmented with explainability mechanisms. Experimental evaluation on Moodle and Canvas datasets shows ${1 5 - 2 5 \%}$ improvement on detection accuracy, ${1 0 - 1 5 \%}$ reduction on false positive and 20-25% improvement on developer productivity among generic LLMs and traditional tools. The proposed approach establishes DS-LLMs as a scalable, interpretable and ethically compliant solution for intelligent vulnerability management for LMS platforms.
