AB  - This paper suggests a self-organizing multi-agent AI system, which would be used to automate a code generation process, refactor and software maintenance in agile development environments. The first one is the increased productivity of developers, the minimization of technical debt, and high-quality software through facilitating AI-driven, sprint-paced development processes. To do this, the study uses the Code Search Net data set as the sole data set, which implies the high-quality large consistent and high-volume training corpus of more than six million code-documentation pairs of a variety of programming languages. This is achieved by preparing the dataset to be advanced learned by preprocessing and annotating it in a strict pipeline of preprocessing and annotation that includes AST parsing, tokenizer standardization, semantic alignment and automated metadata annotation of the dataset. Methodology This paper presents a new three-agent architecture, Planner, Coder and Reviewer Agents, coupled by a common memory state and reinforced by supervised learning, reinforcement learning and behavior cloning. The system is also tested with multi-layered metrics, e.g. code quality, refactoring effectiveness, maintenance accuracy, workflow-level Agile performance indicators. It is shown that the system shows significant performance improvements compared to the leading baseline models, e.g. Codex, CodeT5, and Code BERT. The suggested framework has a BLEU score of 93.4, Refactoring Quality Score of 95.1 and a Bug-Fix Accuracy of 91.7. It also has a very low technical debt, with a Technical Debt Reduction Index of 89.6, and can execute programs more efficiently with an Execution Efficiency Gain of 41.8. These results prove the fact that the multi-agent design gives meaningful contributions to the contextual reasoning, structural code optimization, and long-term maintainability. This novelty is made through sprint conscious, autonomously communicating AI agents that recreate workflows of real developers, allowing self-enhancing, scaling, and productioncapable automation over contemporary Agile software engineering practices.
