AB  - Common Weakness Enumerations (CWEs), Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs), and associated code examples from vulnerability datasets represent a rich corpus of structured knowledge for software vulnerability repair. While Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promise in vulnerability repair, existing methods often lack transparency in how retrieved context informs generated recommendations. This paper presents a graph-based retrieval framework that leverages taxonomic relationships in the CWE hierarchy and CVE associations to guide LLMs in generating repair recommendations. Following XAI principles, we study the impact of graph retrieval strategies from different categories, including the K-Nearest Neighbors Graph Retrieval (KNN), the Metapath Graph Retrieval, and the PageRank Graph Retrieval strategies. These strategies retrieve relevant vulnerability data based on augmented examples representing the relations among CWE and CVE artifacts. Each retrieval result is augmented into the LLM input to improve the code repair recommendation quality. Evaluations across both the BigVul and CVEFixes datasets show consistent gains, with static analysis repair success rates improving from 28% to 63% on BigVul and from 58% to 85% on CVEFixes. To complement SOTA metrics, we incorporate an LLM-as-a-Judge evaluation using five predefined criteria, which include Relevance, Completeness, Correctness, Identification of Vulnerable Code, and Code Guidance. Compared with prior SOTA repair models, our framework achieves higher performance on CodeBLEU, as well as LLM-as-a-Judge evaluations, yielding 0.34 to 0.56 point improvements on all LLM metrics. All models and datasets are open-source, offering a reproducible and extensible framework for future exploration of relationshipaware retrieval and vulnerability repair.
