AB  - This paper comprehensively analyzes communications link performance of RIDU-Sat 1 with a low-cost ground station setup at the Republic of Indonesia Defense University (RIDU). RIDU-Sat 1 is known as the first Indonesia's nano satellite, which collaboratively was developed by RIDU with several partners, carrying APRS payload for educational and research purposes. The satellite operates in the VHF frequency band at 145.925 MHz using GMSK modulation at 1200 baud for Telemetry, Tracking, and Command (TT&C) system. The study evaluates whether such a low-cost setup of a minimalist ground station configuration which comprises software-defined radio (SDR), Yagi antenna, and open-source software tools GNU Radio and YAMCS, can reliably support uninterrupted spacecraft and ground segment communications. The research involves a detailed link budget calculation and Doppler shift characterization for both uplink and downlink paths. The uplink results indicate that even at a low elevation angle of 0°, the system is capable to maintain positive link margin of 5.54 dB, which increases to 19.63 dB at 90°. In comparison, the downlink margins range from 8.24 dB at 0° to 22.33 dB at zenith. The Doppler analysis reveals a maximum frequency deviation of ±3424 Hz, which must be addressed during signal acquisition and tracking. The findings show that a reliable communication can be achieved by utilizing a low-cost equipment and components from commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) supply chains, and it supports the feasibility of nanosatellite development in academic settings and promoting the use of accessible amateur radio infrastructure for space education in emerging countries.
