AB  - This paper investigates the potential of using Large Language Models (LLM) to automate code verification in Android development process. Android’s development process often involves creating a main branch, composed by original Android Open Source Project (AOSP) code from a new Android’s version in addition of manufacturer’s proprietary code, and device specific code. When this branch is stable enough the devices is then launched to the public. When a new device starts the development process, it is forked a new branch with same base code (AOSP + proprietary) plus new device’s specific code. This approach is used to decrease development time but introduces a new problem thus those new branch needs to be modified when a new feature is developed, activated via configuration files, or a bug is found in AOSP or manufacturer’s code. To solve this problem we are proposing a method to use LLM to check parts of code and configuration files across multiple branch with the objective to decrease the development, application, and bug fix time across various device’s branch, and do the application and verification of those features more reliable.
